        <OtherAbstract Type="PIP" Language="eng">
            <AbstractText>Effective pain control can greatly enhance the quality of life of AIDS patients. Persistent AIDS-related pain should be treated in accordance with the three steps in the World Health Organization's &quot;analgesic ladder&quot;: aspirin or paracetamol, codeine or dihydrocodeine, and morphine. The least invasive route of administration should be selected. Because of their side effects, pain control medications should be provided under medical or nursing supervision. Patients should never be left in pain awaiting the next dose, and health workers should not be concerned about the potential for addiction when providing morphine and other opioids to dying patients.</AbstractText>
        </OtherAbstract>
